@page "/privacy"

<PageTitle>Privacy - Clarity Belongs</PageTitle>
<HeadContent>
    <meta name="description" content="Clarity Belongs privacy principles and the data used to provide accounts, monitoring history, alerts, and billing." />
</HeadContent>

<div class="shell public-shell article-shell">
    <header class="hero compact-hero article-hero">
        <div class="eyebrow">PRIVACY</div>
        <h1>Use only what the product needs.</h1>
        <p class="public-lead">Clarity is built around a simple principle: collect and retain the information needed to provide the service, not unrelated personal data.</p>
    </header>

    <article class="article-body legal-copy">
        <section>
            <h2>Information Clarity uses</h2>
            <p>Clarity stores account information such as your email address and display name, your workspace and membership state, the public targets you choose to follow, observation history and change evidence, notification records, and technical information needed to operate the service.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
            <h2>Monitoring data</h2>
            <p>V1 follows public targets. Clarity stores normalized observations and evidence so later changes can be explained. Do not submit private credentials, secrets, or targets that you are not authorized to monitor.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
            <h2>Payments</h2>
            <p>Paid checkout and billing are handled through Stripe-hosted flows. Clarity stores the identifiers and subscription state needed to understand your membership, but the application is not designed to store payment-card details.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
            <h2>Email</h2>
            <p>If email delivery is enabled, Clarity uses your configured notification address to send account recovery and eligible alerts. In-app history exists independently of email delivery.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
            <h2>Retention and deletion</h2>
            <p>Clarity keeps history because history is part of the product. Retention controls and account-deletion tooling will be hardened before broader production scale. Until then, the public V1 should be treated as an early service whose operational policies are still being finalized.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
            <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
            <p>This page will be updated as the public service and its providers are finalized. Material changes should be reflected with a visible effective date before general release.</p>
        </section>
        <p class="legal-note">Draft public-V1 policy. Final legal review, provider details, effective date, and contact information remain part of the release checklist.</p>
    </article>
</div>
